  "final_answer": "**The 20/50 SMA cross on SPY returned +42.9% from 2020 to 2024, less than half of buy-and-hold's +94.6%.** I ran it over 2020-01-02 to 2024-12-31 with $10,000 starting capital. It went fully long on each golden cross (about 99.9% of equity per trade) and exited on each death cross. SPY prices are dividend-adjusted.\n\n**Headline results**\n| Metric | Strategy | SPY buy-and-hold |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total return | +42.9% ($10,000 to $14,286) | +94.6% (295.39 to 574.79) |\n| Annual growth rate | about 7.4% | about 14.2% |\n| Max drawdown | -30.3% | -33.7% (COVID crash, Feb 19 to Mar 23 2020) |\n| Sharpe / Sortino | 0.76 / 1.03 | not computed |\n| Trades | 11 (6 wins, 5 losses, 54.5% win rate) | 1 |\n| Profit factor | 2.13 | n/a |\n| Average win / average loss | +$1,346 / -$758 | n/a |\n| Average holding period | about 79 trading days | n/a |\n\n**Trades**\n1. 2020-04-30 to 2020-10-01: +16.6% (+$1,654)\n2. 2020-10-20 to 2021-10-01: +27.8% (+$3,232, best trade)\n3. 2021-10-28 to 2022-01-25: -5.3%\n4. 2022-04-01 to 2022-05-02: -8.9% (-$1,249, worst trade)\n5. 2022-08-01 to 2022-09-16: -6.1%\n6. 2022-11-11 to 2023-01-03: -4.4%\n7. 2023-01-27 to 2023-03-14: -3.8%\n8. 2023-04-13 to 2023-08-28: +7.1%\n9. 2023-11-20 to 2024-04-26: +12.3%\n10. 2024-05-20 to 2024-08-09: +0.5%\n11. 2024-09-03 to 2024-12-31: +6.6%. This one was closed by the end of the test window, not by a sell signal.\n\n**What stands out**\n- **Nearly all the profit came from the 2020\u201321 recovery.** Trades 1 and 2 made about $4,900; net profit for the whole run was $4,286.\n- **It kept buying false rallies from late 2021 to early 2023.** Trades 3 through 7 were five losses in a row, costing about $3,790. Being mostly out of the market in 2022 did not protect it.\n- **It never got back to its high.** Equity peaked at about $15,500 in early 2022 and was still 8.1% below that at the end of 2024. SPY itself made new highs in January 2024.\n- **Its drawdown was only a little better than buy-and-hold's (-30.3% vs -33.7%), and that edge is partly luck.** The test starts on 2020-01-02 and the strategy needs 50 days of data before it can signal. So it was in cash through the COVID crash by default, not because it read the trend. Had it been in the market beforehand, the sell signal would have come only after most of the fall.\n\n**Reading the tool's own numbers**\n- **Annual return:** the tool reports 10.9% a year, but +42.9% over five years compounds to about 7.4% a year. The 10.9% looks like a simple average of daily returns scaled to a year. The tool's Calmar ratio (0.36) uses that figure; on the 7.4% basis it would be about 0.25.\n- **Performance grade:** the tool gives the strategy an \"F\" while also listing \"High total returns\" as a strength. These contradict each other; I'd go by the numbers rather than either label.\n- **Costs:** the output doesn't say whether commissions or slippage are included. The small amount of cash left out of each trade suggests a small cost may be, but I couldn't confirm it.\n\n**Bottom line:** over this window the strategy clearly underperformed simply holding SPY. It gave up about half the return for only slightly less drawdown, and it was still below its high three years after peaking.\n\nNothing was written to disk; everything above came from the Maverick backtest and price history tools.",
